1. Primary Key
.unique column/field in a table 
.should not contain a duplicate or NULL value 
.identifies each record in a table uniquely 
.helps establish relationships between different tables 
.maintains the integrity of DB by preventing duplicate and null values in the key column 
.can only be one in a table but can be defined on one or more fields - composite primary key 
.can use the AUTO_INCREMENT attribute on this field to automatically add a new value to the column when 
    a new row is created
.makes query execution fast - mysql creates a primary index for the primary key 

>>rules:
    .each value must be unique 
    .no NULL values 
    .primary key value should remain constant throughout the lifespan of the record 
    .should either be system-generated e.g auto-incremented integers OR 
        natural keys e.g existing data that uniquely identifies a record 

>creating PK
.either:
    .create table statement 
    .alter table statement 
.drop PK using alter table statement 
